# help texts for `udiksa <command> -h` (read by ~/.local/bin/udiksa). Format per command:
#   [name]  /  first line = one-line description  /  usage: <arguments>  /  "# HEADING" starts a table
#   "  label  description" = a table row (two spaces between)  /  note: text  /  example: command  explanation
# Tables used: COMMANDS (what to run), ARGUMENTS (what goes in <...> / [...]), OPTIONS (--flags), KEYS / VALUES (accepted settings).
[theme]
Themes: a folder of wallpapers is one palette that recolours the whole rice.
usage: <command> [arguments] [options]
# COMMANDS
  next | prev            Next / previous wallpaper of this theme (wipe transition)
  set <name>             A theme by name: its first picture
  random [filter]        A random picture of a random theme
  apply <picture>        That wallpaper and its theme everywhere
  names [mode]           Theme names, one per line
  current                The theme and wallpaper in use (JSON)
  list                   All themes with previews and colours, for the switcher (JSON)
  reapply                Write the current theme again (after editing its colors.toml)
  colors <picture>       Show a picture's main colours and the theme colours they would give
  import <github link>   Fetch a community theme repo that has a colors.toml and pictures
  corners                Redo the rounded corners of the lock, login and GRUB screens
  thumbs                 Remake the preview pictures
# ARGUMENTS
  <name>                 Theme name, any case, no quotes needed ("Tokyo Night" or tokyo night); see `names`
  <picture>              Path of an image file; its folder decides the theme
  [filter]               dark or light: only those themes; wallpaper: stay in the current theme
  [mode]                 dark or light: only themes of that kind (default: all)
  <github link>          https://github.com/<user>/<repo> of a theme repository
# OPTIONS
  --transition <kind>    set, apply: wipe (default), grow (spreads from --pos; wipe without it), or none (instant)
  --pos <X,Y>            where grow starts, as fractions of the screen (0.5,0.5 = centre) or pixels
note: A theme is a folder in ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/: new folder = new theme. Its colours come from colors.toml in that folder; edit it, then run `reapply`. Pictures are never modified.
example: udiksa theme set "Tokyo Night"  switch theme
example: udiksa theme random dark  a surprise, dark only
example: udiksa theme apply ~/Pictures/me.jpg --transition none  one picture, instantly
[wallpaper]
Change the wallpaper (same theme, or a picture of your own).
usage: next | prev | random | set <picture> | current
# COMMANDS
  next | prev            Next / previous wallpaper of the current theme
  random                 A random wallpaper of the current theme
  set <picture>          Use this picture (its folder decides the theme)
  current                Print the path of the wallpaper in use
# ARGUMENTS
  <picture>              Path of an image file (.jpg, .png, ...)
note: For themes, `udiksa theme set | random` instead. Both use the same colour engine.
example: udiksa wallpaper random  a new look in the same theme
[cursor]
The theme-coloured mouse pointers (Udiksa, Udiksa-Modern).
usage: build [pointer]
usage: greeter <pointer> <size>
# COMMANDS
  build [pointer]        Rebuild the pointer from the current theme colours
  greeter <pointer> <size>  Copy a pointer to the login screen
# ARGUMENTS
  [pointer]              Udiksa (pointy, default) or Udiksa-Modern (rounded); none = the one in use
  <pointer>              Udiksa, Udiksa-Modern or a Bibata theme name
  <size>                 Pointer size in pixels (16-64)
note: A theme change rebuilds the pointer by itself. Pick the pointer and size in Settings > Mouse & keyboard.
[shot]
Screenshots: saved to ~/Pictures/Screenshots, copied, and announced in the notch.
usage: <mode>
# ARGUMENTS
  area                   Drag a rectangle
  window                 Click a window
  screen                 The whole screen (the one with focus)
  edit                   Freeze the screen, pick an area, draw on it (arrows, boxes, text, blur)
note: Click the "Screenshot saved" card to open the picture. In the editor Enter saves and copies, Esc throws it away. File name: Screenshot <date time>.png.
example: udiksa shot area  select a region
[record]
Screen recording, encoded by the GPU. Run it again (or `stop`) to stop.
usage: <mode>
# ARGUMENTS
  screen                 The screen the mouse is on
  region                 Pick an area first
  sound                  The screen plus what you hear and the microphone
  stop                   Stop a running recording (any mode also stops one)
note: Saved in ~/Videos/Recordings as Recording <date>.mp4. The notch shows a blinking dot and the time while it runs; clicking the finished card opens the file or its folder.
[pick]
Colour picker: click anywhere, the hex code is copied and shown in the notch.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Esc cancels. The code (like #ff8a5c) goes to the clipboard.
[toggle]
Show or hide a notch panel, Settings or the theme switcher.
usage: <panel>
# ARGUMENTS
  <panel>                One of the names below
# PANELS
  battery power volume wifi bluetooth media notifications system
  tailscale launcher usage clock clockTimer clockAlarm clockReminder clockEvent
# OTHER TARGETS
  themes                 The theme switcher
  settings               The Settings app
note: A panel opened this way stays open until the mouse has been in the notch and left, or Esc.
example: udiksa toggle system  open or close the system panel
[open]
Open a notch panel (it stays open until you leave it). Close it with `udiksa close`.
usage: <panel>
# ARGUMENTS
  <panel>                A panel name; the list is in `udiksa toggle -h`
note: `udiksa open launcher:clip` (or :emoji) opens the launcher in that menu, which is what `clipboard` and `emoji` do.
[close]
Close the open notch panel.
usage: (no arguments)
[clipboard]
Clipboard history, in the launcher.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Type to search, Enter copies the chosen entry back to the clipboard.
[emoji]
Emoji picker, in the launcher.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Type to search, Enter copies the emoji.
[lock]
Lock the screen.
usage: (no arguments)
[dnd]
Do not disturb: toggles notification pop-ups on and off.
usage: (no arguments)
note: It switches the current state; critical notifications still show.
[osd]
Show a message in the notch.
usage: <icon> <text>
# ARGUMENTS
  <icon>                 One glyph (a Nerd Font icon or an emoji)
  <text>                 The line of text; quote it if it has spaces
example: udiksa osd "󰄬" "Backup done"  an icon and a line of text
[scratch]
App scratchpads: each app lives on its own hidden workspace that a key shows and hides.
usage: <name>
# ARGUMENTS
  sysmon                 btop (system monitor)                   Ctrl+Shift+Esc
  music                  ncspot + cava, or your own app          Super+M
  chat                   your chat app                           Super+D
note: The first press starts the app, later presses show or hide it. Pick the music and chat apps in Settings > Apps; the choice is saved in ~/.config/hypr/local/scratch.json.
[settings]
The back end of the Settings app: saves your choices and applies them live.
usage: get | set <key> <value> | reset [section]
usage: power [<key> <value> | apply]
usage: monitor set <output> '<json>' | many '<json>' | rm <output>
usage: anim <key> <value> | cursor [<pointer> <size>] | shake on|off
usage: startup add|rm <command> | bind add <keys> <command> | bind rm <keys>
# COMMANDS
  get                    All current values (JSON)
  set <key> <value>      Save and apply one option
  reset [section]        Forget saved options and go back to the shipped design
  power                  Lock, screen-off and sleep timers, lid and power-button actions (JSON); with a key and value, change one
  power apply            Write hypridle.conf and the logind settings (first login)
  monitor                A screen's setup, several at once, or back to automatic
  anim                   Animations
  cursor                 Mouse pointer theme and size; no arguments = apply the saved one
  shake on|off           Shake to find the pointer
  startup add|rm         Your own startup commands (run at the next login)
  bind add|rm            Your own shortcuts
# ARGUMENTS
  [section]              look, layout or input (none = everything)
  <output>               "desc:<make model serial>" (follows the screen to any port) or a port name like eDP-1
  '<json>'               {"mode": "2560x1440@144", "scale": 1.25, "position": "auto-right", "transform": 0, "mirror": "", "disabled": false}
  <keys>                 e.g. "SUPER + B"
  <command>              The command to run, e.g. zen-browser
  <pointer> <size>       As in `udiksa cursor -h`; size 16-64
# VALUES for set (bool: true | false | on | off | 1 | 0)
  gaps_in gaps_out                 pixels (whole numbers)
  border_size rounding             pixels (whole numbers)
  active_opacity inactive_opacity  0 to 1
  dim_inactive blur shadow         bool
  animations                       bool
  blur_size blur_passes            whole numbers
  shadow_range shadow_power        whole numbers
  shadow_color                     #rrggbb or #rrggbbaa
  layout                           dwindle | master | scrolling (what Hyprland offers)
  force_split                      dwindle layout: whole number
  split_ratio                      dwindle layout: 0 to 1
  preserve_split                   dwindle layout: bool
  master_side master_new           master layout: text
  master_new_top                   master layout: bool
  master_size                      master layout: 0 to 1
  scroll_direction                 scrolling layout: text
  scroll_width                     scrolling layout: 0 to 1
  kb_layout kb_variant             keyboard layout / variant, e.g. us, dvorak
  repeat_rate                      key repeats per second
  repeat_delay                     milliseconds before repeating
  sensitivity                      -1 to 1
  natural_scroll tap_to_click      touchpad, bool
  disable_while_typing             touchpad, bool
# VALUES for anim
  speed                  50 to 300 (percent)
  windows                popin | slide | fade | gnomed
  workspaces             slide | slidevert | fade | slidefade
  feel                   spring | smooth | crisp
note: Choices are written to ~/.config/hypr/local/ (your personal layer, not in the repo); `reset` removes them. `udiksa settings --full` shows the script's own notes.
[shell]
Start the Quickshell shell at the right physical size for this screen.
usage: [--restart] [quickshell options]
# OPTIONS
  (none)                 Start the shell
  --restart              Stop the running shell first, then start it again
note: The shell is scaled so the notch has the same size in centimetres on any screen. Other options go straight to quickshell (for example -p <path> to run another config).
[ipc]
Call any command of the running shell.
usage: <target> <function> [arguments]
usage: show
# COMMANDS
  show                   List every target and function with its arguments
# ARGUMENTS
  <target>               notch, themes, settings, notifications, modes, osd, lock, agenda, record, power, corners
  <function>             As listed by `show`, e.g. open, toggle, say, lock
  [arguments]            Whatever that function takes (text)
note: A leading `call` is accepted: `udiksa ipc call notch open media` also works.
example: udiksa ipc notch open media  open the media panel
example: udiksa ipc agenda timer 10  start a 10 minute timer
[display]
Screens: cycle or set how an external monitor is used (F9).
usage: [layout]
# ARGUMENTS
  (none)                 The next layout in the cycle below
  extend                 Use both screens side by side
  mirror                 Show the same picture on both
  external               External screen only
  laptop                 Laptop screen only
note: Cycle order: extend, mirror, external only, laptop only. Without an external screen it just says so. The state is forgotten after a reboot (back to normal).
[touchpad]
Switch the laptop touchpad off or on (F10) and show the state in the notch.
usage: (no arguments)
note: The state is kept until reboot, when the touchpad is on again.
[panel-hz]
Set the refresh rate of the laptop's own panel; resolution, position and scale stay.
usage: <hz>
# ARGUMENTS
  <hz>                   A rate the panel offers, e.g. 60 or 240
note: Does nothing without a built-in panel, or when the panel has no such rate. Used by the notch: a low rate on battery, a high one on the charger.
example: udiksa panel-hz 60  save power on battery
[gpu]
One-line GPU facts (never wakes the dGPU).
usage: <what>
# ARGUMENTS
  mode                   GPU mode: Integrated, Hybrid or AsusMuxDgpu
  display                Which GPU drives the laptop panel right now
  dgpu                   NVIDIA GPU power state: active or suspended (plus driver)
  all                    The three above, one per line
note: Switching the mode is done in the notch (System > GPU); it needs a reboot on this setup.
[idle]
Timed actions of hypridle (set the times in Settings > Power).
usage: <action> <source>
# ARGUMENTS
  off                    Screen off
  sleep                  Sleep, then hibernate later if Settings says so
  bat | ac | any         Only on battery, only on the charger, or always
note: Both the battery and the charger timers run; the one for the other power source does nothing.
[defaults]
Set which app opens which kind of file.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Safe to run again. Written to ~/.config/mimeapps.list, per category on purpose (some apps claim far too many types).
[flash]
Write a disk image (.iso, .img, also .xz .gz .zst) to a USB stick or SD card.
usage: [image]
# ARGUMENTS
  [image]                The image file; without it you are asked for a path (the newest image in ~/Downloads is suggested, Tab completes)
note: Lists only removable drives, asks before writing, can verify afterwards, and asks for your password itself. Double-clicking an image in Files opens this.
[xdpi]
Set the DPI for X11 apps from the screen scale.
usage: (no arguments)
note: DPI = 96 x scale, so X11 apps size themselves right on any screen. Runs at login and after a scale change.
[clock]
Open the terminal clock window, in the theme accent.
usage: (no arguments)
[kbd]
ASUS keyboard lighting (does nothing on other machines).
usage: [set <key> <value>... | apply | theme | lit]
# COMMANDS
  (none)                 Saved choices and what this keyboard can do (JSON)
  set <key> <value>      Save and apply; several pairs at once are fine
  apply                  Apply everything saved (first login)
  theme                  Re-apply when "follow_theme" is on
  lit                    The light was just switched on: send the colour again
# KEYS and their values
  brightness             off | low | med | high
  effect                 static | breathe | rainbow-cycle | rainbow-wave | pulse
  colour, colour2        rrggbb, e.g. ff8800 (colour2 = the second colour of breathe)
  follow_theme           true | false (colour = the theme accent, colour2 = its second accent)
  speed                  low | med | high
  direction              up | down | left | right
  boot awake sleep shutdown   true | false (when the light is on at all)
note: Choices are kept in ~/.config/udiksa/keyboard.json (not in the repo). Single-zone keyboards show one colour for the whole keyboard.
example: udiksa kbd set colour ff8800 effect breathe  orange, breathing
[slash]
ASUS lid light (the Slash bar), where the laptop has one.
usage: [set <key> <value>... | apply]
# COMMANDS
  (none)                 Settings and capabilities (JSON; {"capable": false} without a bar)
  set <key> <value>      Change one or more
  apply                  Light or darken the bar for the power source now
# KEYS and their values
  enabled                true | false (the main switch)
  brightness             0 to 255
  interval               0 to 5
  mode                   one of the modes listed in the JSON ("modes")
  boot shutdown sleep battery warning   true | false (when the bar shows)
note: The main switch is kept in ~/.config/udiksa/slash.json; the shell calls `apply` on every plug and unplug.
[onlyoffice]
OnlyOffice, sized for this screen (it is an X11 app).
usage: [files | --new:<kind>]
# ARGUMENTS
  [files]                Documents to open
# OPTIONS
  --new:word|cell|slide|form   Start a new document of that kind
[install]
Run the installer parts.
usage: [part]...
# ARGUMENTS
  arch                   A blank machine only: wipes the disk you pick (run from the Arch USB)
  apps                   Core apps and the optional ones you say yes to
  rice                   Link the dotfiles, login screen, boot splash, hibernation
note: Several parts can be given in order. `udiksa install rice` alone is safe to repeat. Without a part it prints the installer's own help.
example: udiksa install apps rice  on an installed Arch
[update]
Update the system, or the dotfiles themselves.
usage: [target]
# ARGUMENTS
  system                 Official packages, AUR packages, Hyprland plugins (default)
  dots                   git pull the Udiksa repo (fast-forward only), relink, reload and reapply the theme
note: `dots` stops with a message if your copy has local changes that block the pull.
[doctor]
Check that everything the rice needs is in place.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Lists missing commands, the Hyprland and shell state, the udiksa commands and dangling links. Exit code 0 = all good, 1 = something needs attention.
[status]
Version and a quick look at this install: Hyprland, shell, theme, screen, GPU, battery and active modes.
usage: (no arguments)
note: This is what plain `udiksa` shows.
[firstrun]
The setup steps that need a running desktop (runs once after install).
usage: (no arguments)
note: Safe to run by hand at any time. Log: ~/.cache/rice-firstrun.log.
[completion]
Print the bash completion script.
usage: (no arguments)
note: The script is already installed in ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/, so Tab works in new shells.
[version]
The version of this install (the git revision of the Udiksa repo).
usage: (no arguments)
